<Item XName="READ_ME" UserName="READ_ME" Type="Notes">
<Selected><T>Bool</T><D>0</D></Selected>
<Text>&lt;P class=MsoTitle style=&quot;BORDER-TOP: medium none; BORDER-RIGHT: medium none; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 0.75pt solid; PADDING-BOTTOM: 1pt; PADDING-TOP: 0in; PADDING-LEFT: 0in; BORDER-LEFT: medium none; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt; PADDING-RIGHT: 0in&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT size=5 face=Arial&gt;Mixer Intermods&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;TEXT-ALIGN: center; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;Keysight Technologies Example Workspace&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;This example demonstrates use of mixers in Phased Array analysis performing Intermod and Harmonic Calculation.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;EM&gt;SingleTone&lt;/EM&gt; is a system with one input tone. At the output (&lt;EM&gt;SingleTone_Output_Spectrum&lt;/EM&gt; graph) you can observe the two sidebands at 9 GHz and 11 GHz, as well as leakage from input to output at 10 GHz and all the higher order terms and leakages.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT face=&quot;Times New Roman&quot;&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;EM&gt;MultiTone&lt;/EM&gt; is a system with three input tones (configured in &lt;EM&gt;MultiToneConf&lt;/EM&gt; equation). The number of tones in the output spectrum (&lt;EM&gt;MultiTone_Output_Spectrum&lt;/EM&gt;) is much higher than the single tone case. The simulation time has also increased compared to the single tone case.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;The graphs show the results for Channel 1 only. To see the results for other channels, define a tunable variable ChannelIdx and update the expression plotted (double click on the graph and see expression in &lt;STRONG&gt;Variable&lt;/STRONG&gt; field) to use ChannelIdx instead of the fixed value of &quot;1&quot; as the last argument of the extractsweptdata function. Then tune the ChannelIdx variable (from 1 to 64) in the &lt;STRONG&gt;Tune&lt;/STRONG&gt; window and see the graphs update to show results for a different channel. You do &lt;STRONG&gt;NOT&lt;/STRONG&gt; have to rerun the simulations to see the results for different channels, so make sure the checkboxes next to the analyses names in the &lt;STRONG&gt;Analyses To Run&lt;/STRONG&gt; area in &lt;STRONG&gt;Tune&lt;/STRONG&gt; window are &lt;STRONG&gt;NOT&lt;/STRONG&gt; checked.&lt;/P&gt;</Text>
